        "number": 1,
        "id": "sup-03fp-7kqa-0001",
        "message": "First repair request. Verified artifact hash a98c8caf33d6857ed69a76b0a93ff0ac40bf1c7f8e38863072a405b7cd8a88a8 matches state.json.\n\nDETECTIVE FINDING - real app defect (verified by byte-level simulation of the exact copy code):\n\nWhen you resize the canvas to a LARGER width than the current canvas, existing pixel data is scrambled/lost. Both onCanvasSizeChange() and applyResize() (the resize modal) contain the same bug.\n\nRoot cause: both functions assign state.width = w and state.height = h BEFORE the pixel copy loop. The copy loop then reads source pixels with index (y * state.width + x) * 4, but state.width is now the NEW width, not the old width. The old pixel array is still the OLD width wide, so the source stride is wrong. When new width > old width and there is more than one row, pixels are read from the wrong source rows.\n\nEvidence: I simulated the exact copy loop on a 4x4 grid where each pixel held a unique marker (pixel x,y => r=x*10, g=y*10). Resizing 4x4 -> 8x8:\n- Correct logic (stride = old width 4): pixel at new (0,1) has g = 10. PASS.\n- App logic (stride = new width 8): pixel at new (0,1) has g = 20. FAIL - data scrambled.\n\nExpected behavior: enlarging the canvas should preserve all original pixels in their correct positions in the top-left region and only add empty (background) cells. Currently enlarging shifts/mixes rows so the drawing is corrupted.\n\nAlso note: the copy loop reads state.width inside the loop even though it already stored w; capture the old width in a local variable (e.g. const oldW = state.width) and use oldW as the source stride, or do the copy before reassigning state.width/state.height.\n\nThis affects the \"editable canvas size\" requirement and the resize modal. Fix the source-stride index in BOTH onCanvasSizeChange and applyResize. Please also add a test that resizes up and down and checks original pixels are preserved.\n\nDo not change any other verified-good behavior.\n",
        "reason": "verified byte-level defect: enlarging the canvas scrambles existing pixels in onCanvasSizeChange/applyResize (source stride uses new width instead of old width). Evidence and expected outcome in feedback-1.txt.",
        "interrupt": true,
        "proposedUTC": "2026-11-19T00:27:43.536835+00:00",
        "acceptedUTC": "2026-11-19T00:27:43.536862+00:00",
        "elapsedSeconds": 127.325,
        "workerPID": 39972,
        "interrupted": false,
        "deliveredUTC": "2026-11-19T00:27:43.581075+00:00",
        "deliveredElapsedSeconds": 127.325
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "id": "sup-03fp-7kqa-0004",
        "message": "Two points in this message.\n\n1) CONFIRMED - the resize pixel-scramble defect you fixed is now verified correct. I simulated the exact new copy loop (source stride = old width) for enlarge-W, enlarge-both, shrink, shrink-W, equal, and enlarge-H-only. In every case original pixels are preserved positionally. Good work on that fix; you can stop worrying about it.\n\n2) NEW FINDING - real defect in the sidebar \"Preset Sizes\" panel (part of the editable-canvas-size requirement). The preset buttons are ignored.\n\nRoot cause: buildPresets wires each preset button to onclick = () => { state.width = w; state.height = h; onCanvasSizeChange(); }. But onCanvasSizeChange does not read the target size from state - it reads it from the DOM inputs #canvas-w and #canvas-h. The preset click never updates those inputs, so onCanvasSizeChange always uses whatever the header inputs currently hold (default 32x32). Clicking \"64x64\" therefore resizes to 32x32, not 64x64.\n\nEvidence (byte-level simulation of the real handler path): starting from DOM inputs 32x32, clicking the 64x64 preset sets state.width=64 but the handler reads the DOM inputs (still 32) and resizes to 32x32. FAIL.\n\nExpected behavior: clicking a preset button (16x16, 32x32, 48x48, 64x64, 128x128, 256x256) should resize the canvas to that preset size. The simplest correct fix is to make the preset button also write the header inputs before calling the resize handler, e.g. set the #canvas-w / #canvas-h values to w and h first, or call a shared resize(oldW, oldH, w, h) helper directly. Either way the clicked preset size must be the size applied.\n\nPlease fix the preset buttons and add a test that clicks a preset and checks the resulting canvas dimensions match the preset. Do not otherwise change verified-good behavior.\n",
        "reason": "resize fix verified correct (stop worrying about it). New verified defect: sidebar Preset Sizes buttons are ignored - onCanvasSizeChange reads header DOM inputs which the preset click never updates, so clicking 64x64 resizes to 32x32. Evidence + expected outcome in feedback-2.txt.",
        "interrupt": true,
        "proposedUTC": "2026-11-19T00:33:46.136104+00:00",
        "acceptedUTC": "2026-11-19T00:33:46.136115+00:00",
